xt","text":"Every cycle of a functional graph, and for each vertex its tail and the cycle it reaches, in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Flajolet and Odlyzko’s terms: walking from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":", the first vertex on a cycle is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"root(of: v)"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tailLength(of: v)"},{"type":"text","text":" steps away, on a cycle of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cycleLength(of: v)"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertices. For a permutation, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cycles"},{"type":"text","text":" is its cycle decomposition and every tail is zero."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph":{"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph","type":"topic","title":"FunctionalGraph","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FunctionalGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"FunctionalGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The graph of a function f on the vertices "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<n"},{"type":"text","text":": every vertex "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" has exactly one out-edge,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v → f(v)"},{"type":"text","text":", and a fixed point is a self-loop. Also called a successor graph."}],"url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","type":"topic","role":"collection","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs","title":"FunctionalGraphs","kind":"symbol"}}}